// Domain 1: Detection (16%)
// Schema: id, d (domain), q ("scenario\n\nquestion"), o (options), a (indices of correct options), r (one rationale per option).
// Options are shuffled at runtime, so rationales never refer to option letters.
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id: "d1-01", d: 1,
  q: "A company has 60 AWS accounts in an organization in AWS Organizations and creates about five new accounts each month. Amazon GuardDuty is enabled only in the management account today. The security team must enable GuardDuty in every existing and future account in all active Regions, and must review all findings from a dedicated security tooling account. The management account must not be used for daily security operations.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["From the management account, designate the security tooling account as the GuardDuty delegated administrator in each Region. Set auto-enable to all accounts there.",
      "From the management account, designate the security tooling account as the GuardDuty delegated administrator in each Region. In that account, set auto-enable to new accounts only.",
      "Keep the management account as the GuardDuty administrator account. Set auto-enable to all accounts in the organization, and give the security team a role in the management account.",
      "From the management account, designate the security tooling account as the GuardDuty delegated administrator in the home Region only. In that account, set auto-enable to all accounts."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["GuardDuty is a Regional service, so the delegated administrator is designated in each Region. The setting that auto-enables all accounts turns on GuardDuty for existing members and for accounts that join later, and all findings appear in the security tooling account.",
      "The new-accounts-only setting enables GuardDuty for accounts that join later, but it leaves the existing 60 accounts without GuardDuty.",
      "This covers every account, but the security team would work in the management account, which the company does not allow.",
      "A delegated administrator in one Region manages GuardDuty only in that Region, so the other active Regions stay unprotected."] },

{ id: "d1-02", d: 1,
  q: "A company uses AWS Security Hub CSPM for all accounts in its organization in four AWS Regions, with a delegated administrator account. Analysts in the delegated administrator account switch between Regions to review findings, which slows down triage. The analysts must review and update findings from all accounts and all four Regions in one Region, and workflow status changes that they make must also appear in the Region where each finding was created.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["In the delegated administrator account, choose one of the four Regions as the home Region. Link the other three Regions to it for cross-Region aggregation.",
      "In the organization's management account, choose one of the four Regions as the home Region. Link the other three Regions to it for cross-Region aggregation.",
      "In the delegated administrator account, create an Amazon EventBridge rule in each linked Region. Forward new findings to an event bus in the Region that the analysts use.",
      "In the delegated administrator account, create an insight in each of the four Regions that groups findings by account. Add the four insights to one shared dashboard."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Cross-Region aggregation in the administrator account brings findings from all member accounts in the linked Regions into the home Region. Updates to findings in the home Region are replicated back to the Region where each finding was created.",
      "The management account is not the Security Hub CSPM administrator here. Aggregation that it configures would include only its own findings, not those of the member accounts.",
      "Forwarded events are copies outside the Security Hub CSPM workflow, so status changes made on them do not reach the original findings.",
      "Insights in each Region still keep the findings in four places, and the analysts would still switch Regions to update them."] },

{ id: "d1-03", d: 1,
  q: "A company's security operations team must receive an email within 5 minutes when Amazon GuardDuty produces a finding with a severity of 7.0 or higher in the company's production account in us-east-1. Findings with lower severity must not generate email. AWS Security Hub CSPM is not enabled in the account, and the team already has an Amazon SNS topic with a confirmed email subscription in us-east-1.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an Amazon EventBridge rule with a pattern that matches source aws.guardduty and a numeric detail.severity of 7 or higher. Set the SNS topic as the target.",
      "Create an Amazon EventBridge rule with a pattern that matches source aws.guardduty and the detail-type GuardDuty Finding. Set the SNS topic as the target.",
      "Create an Amazon EventBridge rule with a pattern that matches source aws.securityhub and a severity label of HIGH or CRITICAL. Set the SNS topic as the target.",
      "Set the GuardDuty findings export frequency to 15 minutes with an S3 destination. Configure an S3 event notification on the bucket that publishes to the SNS topic."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["GuardDuty sends each finding to EventBridge in near real time. A numeric matching pattern on detail.severity selects only findings of 7.0 or higher, and the SNS topic delivers the email within minutes.",
      "This rule matches every GuardDuty finding, so low-severity findings would also generate email.",
      "Security Hub CSPM is not enabled in the account, so no Security Hub CSPM events exist for the rule to match.",
      "Exports run on a schedule and contain findings of every severity, so the email would be late and unfiltered."] },

{ id: "d1-04", d: 1,
  q: "A company sends AWS CloudTrail management events from its account to an Amazon CloudWatch Logs log group. The security policy requires an alert within minutes whenever an IAM user signs in to the AWS Management Console successfully without MFA. Federated users who sign in through AWS IAM Identity Center must not cause alerts, and failed sign-in attempts are monitored by a separate process.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create a metric filter for ConsoleLogin events where userIdentity.type is IAMUser, the result is Success, and MFAUsed is not Yes. Create an alarm on the metric that notifies an Amazon SNS topic.",
      "Create a metric filter for all ConsoleLogin events where MFAUsed is not Yes, with no condition on the identity type or the result. Create an alarm on the metric that notifies an Amazon SNS topic.",
      "Create a metric filter for ConsoleLogin events where userIdentity.type is IAMUser, the result is Failure, and MFAUsed is not Yes. Create an alarm on the metric that notifies an Amazon SNS topic.",
      "Turn on the AWS Config managed rule mfa-enabled-for-iam-console-access. Send compliance changes for the rule to an Amazon SNS topic through an Amazon EventBridge rule."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["ConsoleLogin events record the identity type, the result, and whether MFA was used. Filtering on all three alerts only on successful IAM user sign-ins without MFA, which is the control that the AWS security standards recommend.",
      "Without conditions on the identity type and the result, the filter also counts federated sign-ins and failed attempts, which must not cause alerts.",
      "This filter counts failed sign-ins, which another process handles, and misses the successful sign-ins without MFA that the policy is about.",
      "The rule reports whether IAM users with console passwords have an MFA device. It does not report individual sign-ins, and a user with a device can still sign in without using it."] },

{ id: "d1-05", d: 1,
  q: "A company stores data in about 3,000 Amazon S3 buckets across 40 accounts in AWS Organizations, and teams create new buckets every week. The security team must keep a current view of which buckets are likely to contain personal and financial data, without creating and scheduling a discovery job for each bucket. Results for all accounts must be visible in a security tooling account.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Designate the security tooling account as the Amazon Macie delegated administrator. Enable Macie for all member accounts, and turn on automated sensitive data discovery.",
      "Designate the security tooling account as the Amazon Macie delegated administrator. Enable Macie for all member accounts, and run a one-time sensitive data discovery job for all current buckets.",
      "Enable Amazon Macie separately in each member account, and turn on automated sensitive data discovery in each account. Review the results in each account's Macie console.",
      "Designate the security tooling account as the Amazon Macie delegated administrator. Use the bucket inventory and policy findings that Macie generates for each bucket as the view."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Automated sensitive data discovery continually samples objects across all buckets, including new ones, and reports which buckets are likely to hold sensitive data. The delegated administrator sees the results for every member account.",
      "A one-time job covers only the buckets that exist when it runs, so the view goes out of date as teams create buckets.",
      "Without a delegated administrator, results stay in 40 separate accounts instead of one security tooling account.",
      "Bucket inventory and policy findings describe settings such as public access and encryption. They do not show what data the objects contain."] },

{ id: "d1-06", d: 1,
  q: "A company must centralize AWS CloudTrail management events, VPC Flow Logs, and Amazon Route 53 Resolver query logs from 25 accounts in four Regions. The data must be stored in Amazon S3 buckets that the company owns, normalized to one open, standard schema, and queryable by Amazon Athena and by a third-party SIEM. Accounts that are added to the organization must be included automatically.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Enable Amazon Security Lake from a delegated administrator account in the four Regions. Add the three AWS log sources for the whole organization, and add the SIEM as a subscriber.",
      "Enable Amazon Security Lake from a delegated administrator account in the four Regions. Add the three AWS log sources for the 25 current account IDs, and add the SIEM as a subscriber.",
      "Create an organization trail, VPC flow logs, and Resolver query logging that deliver to one S3 bucket. Define AWS Glue tables for each format, and share the bucket with the SIEM.",
      "Enable AWS Security Hub CSPM in all accounts with cross-Region aggregation. Send the aggregated findings to the SIEM through an Amazon EventBridge rule in the home Region."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Security Lake collects these AWS log sources into S3 buckets in the company's account and normalizes them to the Open Cybersecurity Schema Framework (OCSF). Enabling sources for the whole organization includes new accounts, and subscribers such as a SIEM can read the data.",
      "Listing the 25 current account IDs leaves out accounts that join the organization later.",
      "This stores the raw logs, but each source keeps its own format, so the data is not normalized to one schema.",
      "Security Hub CSPM findings are summaries of issues, not the CloudTrail, flow log, and DNS records that must be centralized."] },

{ id: "d1-07", d: 1,
  q: "A security team has packaged 14 AWS Config rules and their remediation actions to collect PCI DSS evidence. AWS Config is recording in all 70 accounts of the company's organization, and the team administers AWS Config from a delegated administrator account. Every account, including accounts that join later, must be evaluated against the same rules with the same remediation, and member account administrators must not be able to delete the rules.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Deploy the rules and remediation actions as an organization conformance pack from the delegated administrator account.",
      "Deploy the rules and remediation actions as a conformance pack in each member account by using an AWS CloudFormation StackSet with automatic deployment.",
      "Deploy the rules as organization AWS Config rules from the delegated administrator account. Configure the remediation actions in each member account after it joins.",
      "Create an AWS Config aggregator in the delegated administrator account. Add the 14 rules to the aggregator, and let the aggregator evaluate every source account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An organization conformance pack deploys rules and remediation actions as one unit to all member accounts, including accounts that join later. Member accounts cannot modify or delete the rules that it creates.",
      "Conformance packs that are deployed inside each member account belong to that account, so member administrators can delete them.",
      "Organization rules deploy the rules everywhere, but configuring remediation in each new account by hand breaks the requirement for the same remediation everywhere.",
      "An aggregator collects compliance data from source accounts. It cannot hold rules or evaluate resources in other accounts."] },

{ id: "d1-08", d: 1,
  q: "A company runs 800 Amazon EC2 instances that are managed by AWS Systems Manager. A host intrusion detection agent must be installed and running on every instance, including instances that Auto Scaling launches later. Engineers sometimes stop the agent during troubleshooting and forget to restart it. Any instance where the agent is not running must be corrected within 30 minutes without manual action.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a State Manager association that installs and starts the agent. Target all managed instances, and schedule the association to run every 30 minutes.",
      "Create a State Manager association that installs and starts the agent. Target the instance IDs that exist today, and schedule the association to run every 30 minutes.",
      "Run a Run Command document that installs and starts the agent on all managed instances. Create a maintenance window that runs the command once each week.",
      "Use Systems Manager Inventory to collect the installed applications every 30 minutes. Report instances that do not have the agent as noncompliant in AWS Config."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A State Manager association keeps managed instances in a defined state and runs on a schedule as often as every 30 minutes. Targeting all managed instances includes new ones, and each run starts the agent again if an engineer stopped it.",
      "Instances that Auto Scaling launches later are not in a list of today's instance IDs, so they would never get the agent.",
      "A weekly run can leave the agent stopped for days, which misses the 30-minute requirement.",
      "Inventory and AWS Config only report the problem. Nothing starts the agent again, and installed is not the same as running."] },

{ id: "d1-09", d: 1,
  q: "A company's security team must continuously evaluate 30 accounts against AWS security best practices, including checks for S3 buckets without Block Public Access, public Amazon EBS snapshots, and IAM users with unused credentials. Each account must have a security score, and the team must view all results from a delegated administrator account without writing custom checks.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Enable AWS Security Hub CSPM for the organization from the delegated administrator account. Enable the AWS Foundational Security Best Practices standard in all accounts.",
      "Enable AWS Config in all accounts, and deploy the Operational Best Practices for IAM conformance pack. Use the conformance pack compliance score for each account.",
      "Enable Amazon Inspector for the organization from the delegated administrator account. Use the Amazon Inspector risk score of each account as the security score.",
      "Enable AWS Security Hub CSPM for the organization from the delegated administrator account. Create custom insights for each best practice, and calculate the score from them."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The AWS Foundational Security Best Practices standard has managed controls for these checks and many more. Security Hub CSPM calculates a security score for each enabled standard, and the delegated administrator sees results for all accounts.",
      "The IAM conformance pack covers IAM practices only. It does not check S3 Block Public Access or public EBS snapshots.",
      "Amazon Inspector scores software vulnerabilities on compute resources. It does not evaluate configurations such as public snapshots.",
      "Insights group existing findings. Without an enabled standard there are no control findings to group, and a self-built score is custom work."] },

{ id: "d1-10", d: 1,
  q: "A company runs 12 Amazon EKS clusters on Amazon EC2 nodes and already uses Amazon GuardDuty with EKS Protection for Kubernetes audit logs. The security team must also detect threats inside running containers, such as a process that connects to a cryptocurrency mining pool or a reverse shell. The team does not want to install or update a security agent on the clusters by hand.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Enable GuardDuty Runtime Monitoring for Amazon EKS. Turn on automated agent configuration for EKS, and let GuardDuty manage the security agent add-on.",
      "Enable GuardDuty Runtime Monitoring for Amazon EKS. Install the GuardDuty security agent add-on on each cluster, and update it when new versions are released.",
      "Keep EKS Protection as the only GuardDuty feature for the clusters. Add a GuardDuty threat list that contains the IP addresses of known mining pools.",
      "Enable GuardDuty Malware Protection for EC2 on the worker nodes. Configure the malware scans to start when GuardDuty generates a finding for a node."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Runtime Monitoring uses a security agent to watch process, file, and network activity inside containers, and it detects mining and reverse shells. With automated agent configuration, GuardDuty deploys and updates the agent add-on itself.",
      "This detects the threats, but installing and updating the add-on on every cluster is the manual work that the team wants to avoid.",
      "Kubernetes audit logs record API server activity, not processes inside containers, and a threat list misses pools that are not on it.",
      "Malware Protection scans the EBS volumes for malicious files after a finding. It does not watch processes or connections while containers run."] },

{ id: "d1-11", d: 1,
  q: "A company logs all management events with an AWS CloudTrail trail. The security team must be notified when the rate of write management API calls, such as RunInstances or DeleteBucket, becomes unusual compared with the account's normal activity. The team does not want to set or maintain a threshold or a metric for each API, and API calls that fail with access errors are already monitored separately.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Enable CloudTrail Insights on the trail with the API call rate insight type. Create an Amazon EventBridge rule that sends Insights events to an Amazon SNS topic.",
      "Enable CloudTrail Insights on the trail with the API error rate insight type. Create an Amazon EventBridge rule that sends Insights events to an Amazon SNS topic.",
      "Create a CloudWatch metric filter for each write API in the trail's log group. Create a CloudWatch anomaly detection alarm on each of the new metrics.",
      "Enable Amazon GuardDuty in the account. Create an Amazon EventBridge rule that sends findings of the Discovery and Impact types to an Amazon SNS topic."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudTrail Insights learns the normal rate of write management API calls and records an Insights event when the rate is unusual, with no per-API thresholds. EventBridge can deliver the Insights events to the team.",
      "The API error rate insight type looks at calls that fail, which another process already covers, and it does not cover unusual call volume.",
      "Anomaly detection removes static thresholds, but the team would still create and maintain a metric and an alarm for every API.",
      "GuardDuty finds specific threats such as unusual API behavior by a principal. It does not report the overall rate of write calls against the account's baseline."] },

{ id: "d1-12", d: 1,
  q: "Amazon GuardDuty reports an UnauthorizedAccess:IAMUser/InstanceCredentialExfiltration.OutsideAWS finding in a production account. The finding names an IAM role that is attached to an Amazon EC2 instance in an Auto Scaling group behind an Application Load Balancer. The instance still allows IMDSv1, and a server-side request forgery (SSRF) flaw was reported last week in the web application that runs on the instance.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely explanation for this finding?",
  o: ["An attacker obtained the role's temporary credentials from the instance metadata service. The attacker is using them from an IP address outside AWS.",
      "An attacker obtained the role's temporary credentials from the instance metadata service. The attacker is using them from an EC2 instance in another AWS account.",
      "The instance is sending traffic to an IP address outside AWS that appears on a GuardDuty threat list. The address belongs to a command and control server.",
      "An IAM user's long-term access key that is stored on the instance is being used. The calls come from an IP address outside AWS that the account has not used before."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["This finding means that credentials issued to the instance through its role are being used from an external IP address. An SSRF flaw with IMDSv1 is a common way for attackers to read those credentials from the metadata service.",
      "Use of instance credentials from another AWS account produces the InsideAWS variant of this finding, not the OutsideAWS variant.",
      "Traffic to a known command and control server produces a Backdoor finding for the instance, not a credential exfiltration finding for the role.",
      "The finding concerns temporary credentials of an instance role. Use of an IAM user's long-term key produces different finding types."] },

{ id: "d1-13", d: 1,
  q: "A company uses AWS Security Hub CSPM across 120 accounts with a delegated administrator account. The on-call team must see findings from 10 production accounts first, so every new finding from those accounts must be set to CRITICAL severity and given a note when it is created. Findings from all other accounts must keep their original severity, and the team does not want to run custom code.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["In the delegated administrator account, create an automation rule that matches the 10 production account IDs. Set it to update the severity label to CRITICAL and add the note.",
      "In each of the 10 production accounts, create an automation rule that matches findings from that account. Set the rule to update the severity label to CRITICAL and to add the note.",
      "In the delegated administrator account, create a custom action that sets the severity label to CRITICAL. Ask the on-call team to run the action on each new production finding.",
      "In the delegated administrator account, create an insight that filters on the 10 production account IDs. Add the insight to the on-call team's dashboard in the console."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Automation rules update findings automatically when they are created or updated, based on criteria such as the account ID. Only the administrator account can create them, and they can change the severity label and add a note without custom code.",
      "Automation rules are created in the administrator account. Member accounts cannot create them.",
      "A custom action needs a person to run it on each finding, so new findings would not be set to CRITICAL when they are created.",
      "An insight groups findings for viewing. It does not change their severity or add notes."] },

{ id: "d1-14", d: 1,
  q: "A company stores payroll reports in Amazon S3 buckets in three accounts that use Amazon GuardDuty. The security team must be alerted when an IAM principal accesses the objects in an unusual way, such as GetObject calls from a known malicious IP address or an abnormal volume of requests from one principal. The team does not want to configure or pay for AWS CloudTrail S3 data events in its own trails.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Enable the S3 Protection feature of GuardDuty in the three accounts. Send the resulting S3 data access findings to the team through an Amazon EventBridge rule.",
      "Enable GuardDuty Malware Protection for S3 on the payroll buckets. Send the resulting malware scan findings to the team through an Amazon EventBridge rule.",
      "Add S3 data events for the payroll buckets to the trails in the three accounts. Add a GuardDuty trusted IP list, and alert on findings for other addresses.",
      "Enable Amazon Macie for the payroll buckets in the three accounts. Send the Macie policy findings for the buckets to the team through an Amazon EventBridge rule."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["S3 Protection lets GuardDuty monitor S3 data events for suspicious access, such as calls from malicious IP addresses or unusual request patterns. GuardDuty reads these events itself, so the company does not add data events to its trails.",
      "Malware Protection for S3 scans newly uploaded objects for malware. It does not analyze how principals access existing objects.",
      "This adds the data events that the team does not want to pay for, and a trusted IP list suppresses findings instead of finding unusual access.",
      "Macie policy findings report bucket settings such as public access or encryption changes. They do not describe unusual object access by principals."] },

{ id: "d1-15", d: 1,
  q: "A company has 50 accounts in AWS Organizations and creates new accounts each month. The security team must scan all Amazon EC2 instances, Amazon ECR container images, and AWS Lambda functions for software vulnerabilities in every account, including new accounts, without running scanners that the team installs and maintains. All findings must be visible in a security tooling account.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Designate the security tooling account as the Amazon Inspector delegated administrator. Activate EC2, ECR, and Lambda scanning for all members, and auto-activate new members.",
      "Designate the security tooling account as the Amazon Inspector delegated administrator. Activate EC2, ECR, and Lambda scanning for all members that are in the organization today.",
      "Activate Amazon Inspector in each account by using an AWS CloudFormation StackSet with automatic deployment. Review the findings in the Amazon Inspector console of each account.",
      "Designate the security tooling account as the Amazon Inspector delegated administrator. Activate EC2 and ECR scanning for all members, and rely on ECR basic scanning for Lambda."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Amazon Inspector scans EC2 instances, ECR images, and Lambda functions for vulnerabilities. The delegated administrator can activate scanning for current members and automatically for new members, and it sees all findings in one place.",
      "Without automatic activation, accounts created next month would not be scanned until someone activates them.",
      "Activation in each account works, but the findings stay in 50 separate consoles instead of one security tooling account.",
      "ECR basic scanning covers container images in ECR. It does not scan Lambda functions, so Lambda scanning must be activated in Amazon Inspector."] },

{ id: "d1-16", d: 1,
  q: "A company uses AWS Organizations with a dedicated log archive account. The company must record management events from all current and future accounts in every AWS Region. The log files must be encrypted with a customer managed AWS KMS key that the security team controls, and auditors must be able to prove that no log file was changed or deleted after delivery.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create an organization trail from the management account that applies to all Regions and delivers to a bucket in the log archive account.",
      "Turn on log file integrity validation for the trail, and set SSE-KMS with the customer managed key that allows CloudTrail to use it.",
      "Create a trail in every account by using a StackSet, and point every trail to one S3 bucket in the log archive account.",
      "Turn on S3 Versioning and MFA delete on the log bucket, and give the auditors these settings as proof of integrity.",
      "Create an organization trail that applies only to the home Region, and turn on global service events for that trail."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["An organization trail logs events for every account in the organization, including accounts added later, and an all-Regions trail covers every Region.",
      "Log file integrity validation creates signed digest files that prove whether log files were changed or deleted. SSE-KMS with the customer managed key meets the encryption requirement, and the key policy must allow CloudTrail.",
      "Trails in each account must be added for every new account, which an organization trail does automatically.",
      "Versioning and MFA delete make deletion harder, but they do not give auditors a verifiable proof that files were not changed.",
      "A single-Region trail records global service events but misses management events in the other Regions."] },

{ id: "d1-17", d: 1,
  q: "A company has an AWS CloudTrail trail that logs management events for its account. The company must also log every object-level read and write in one Amazon S3 bucket that stores payroll files. The account has 400 other buckets with heavy traffic, so CloudTrail charges must not include data events for any other bucket, including buckets that teams create later.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Add an advanced event selector for S3 data events to the trail. Set a resources.ARN condition that starts with the payroll bucket's ARN followed by a slash.",
      "Add an advanced event selector for S3 data events to the trail. Set resources.type to AWS::S3::Object with no condition on resources.ARN.",
      "Add an advanced event selector for S3 data events to the trail. Set a resources.ARN condition that equals the payroll bucket's ARN with no slash.",
      "Turn on S3 server access logging for the payroll bucket only. Deliver the access logs to the trail's bucket, and let CloudTrail process them as data events."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Object ARNs have the form of the bucket ARN, a slash, and the key. A StartsWith condition on the bucket ARN followed by a slash matches every object in the payroll bucket and nothing else.",
      "Without an ARN condition, the selector logs object activity in all 401 buckets, which is the cost that the company wants to avoid.",
      "An object ARN never equals the bucket ARN, so this condition would not match the object-level reads and writes.",
      "Server access logs are a separate log type. CloudTrail does not read or process them as data events."] },

{ id: "d1-18", d: 1,
  q: "Auditors must run SQL queries over 7 years of AWS CloudTrail management events from all 90 accounts in a company's organization, including accounts added later. The company does not want to build an extract, transform, and load (ETL) pipeline, add table partitions, or run database or search clusters. The auditors must run their queries from a delegated administrator account.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an organization event data store in CloudTrail Lake with 7-year retention. Run the auditors' SQL queries from the delegated administrator account.",
      "Create an event data store in CloudTrail Lake in each of the 90 accounts with a 7-year retention period. Run the auditors' SQL queries in each account.",
      "Deliver an organization trail to Amazon S3. Create an Amazon Athena table for the logs, and run MSCK REPAIR TABLE each day to load the new partitions.",
      "Stream the events from an organization trail to an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain. Keep 7 years of indexes, and run SQL queries with the OpenSearch SQL plugin."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudTrail Lake stores events in managed event data stores that can be queried with SQL, with no ETL or partitions to manage. An organization event data store collects events from all accounts, including new ones, and a delegated administrator can query it.",
      "Separate event data stores keep the data in 90 places, and new accounts would need their own stores.",
      "Loading partitions every day is the table maintenance that the company wants to avoid.",
      "An OpenSearch domain is a cluster that the company would size and run for 7 years of data."] },

{ id: "d1-19", d: 1,
  q: "Applications in 30 workload accounts write logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs. The security team must copy these logs to a dedicated logging account within minutes of each event, and the copies must survive if an attacker deletes log groups in a workload account. Administrators in the workload accounts must not be able to read or change the copies in the logging account.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create a CloudWatch Logs destination in the logging account that delivers to Amazon Data Firehose, with a policy that allows the workload accounts.",
      "Create a subscription filter on each workload log group that sends its log events to the destination in the logging account.",
      "Configure CloudWatch cross-account observability with the logging account as the monitoring account for the 30 workload accounts.",
      "Create an export task each day in each workload account that writes the log groups to an S3 bucket in the logging account.",
      "Encrypt each log group in the workload accounts with an AWS KMS key that the logging account owns and controls."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["A destination in the logging account receives log events from other accounts and delivers them to storage that the workload accounts cannot reach. Its access policy controls which accounts can send to it.",
      "Subscription filters stream log events in near real time to the destination, so copies arrive within minutes and stay in the logging account if the source log group is deleted.",
      "Cross-account observability lets the logging account view the source logs, but it does not copy them, so deleted log groups would be gone.",
      "Daily export tasks do not deliver copies within minutes of each event.",
      "Encryption with a key from another account controls who can read the source logs. It does not create copies in the logging account."] },

{ id: "d1-20", d: 1,
  q: "A company must collect the /var/log/secure file from 200 Amazon Linux instances into Amazon CloudWatch Logs to investigate failed SSH sign-in attempts. The instances are managed by AWS Systems Manager, and new instances launch every day from the same AMI. The collection settings must be kept in one place, and a change to them must reach every instance.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Store a CloudWatch agent configuration that collects /var/log/secure in Parameter Store. Use a State Manager association to install the agent and apply it on all instances.",
      "Store a CloudWatch agent configuration that collects /var/log/secure in Parameter Store. Use Run Command once to install the agent and apply the configuration on the running instances.",
      "Install the CloudWatch agent in the AMI with a local configuration file that collects /var/log/secure. Build a new AMI and replace the instances whenever the settings change.",
      "Store a CloudWatch agent configuration that collects memory and disk metrics in Parameter Store. Use a State Manager association to install and apply it on all instances."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The CloudWatch agent sends log files to CloudWatch Logs. A configuration in Parameter Store is the one place for the settings, and a State Manager association applies it to every managed instance, including new ones.",
      "Run Command runs once, so instances that launch tomorrow would not get the agent or the configuration.",
      "A configuration baked into the AMI is not kept in one place for running instances, and every change needs a new AMI and a replacement of all instances.",
      "This configuration collects metrics, not the /var/log/secure file, so the sign-in records would not reach CloudWatch Logs."] },

{ id: "d1-21", d: 1,
  q: "A company's security team must investigate which Amazon EC2 instances in a VPC communicate with suspicious external IP addresses. The VPC has no flow logs yet. Analysts need to run SQL queries over 12 months of traffic records from the VPC, and most queries filter on a single day.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Create a VPC flow log that publishes to Amazon S3 in Apache Parquet format with hourly partitions. Query the flow logs by using Amazon Athena.",
      "Create a VPC flow log that publishes to Amazon S3 in plain text format with the default fields. Query the flow logs by using Amazon Athena.",
      "Create a VPC flow log that publishes to Amazon CloudWatch Logs with a 12-month retention period. Query the flow logs by using CloudWatch Logs Insights.",
      "Enable Amazon GuardDuty for the account. Query the VPC flow log records that GuardDuty analyzes by using Amazon Athena."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Parquet is a compressed columnar format, so it costs less to store and Athena scans less data per query. Hourly partitions let a query for one day read only that day's files.",
      "Plain text works with Athena, but queries scan more data and storage is larger than with Parquet.",
      "Ingesting and storing 12 months of flow logs in CloudWatch Logs costs more than storing them in Amazon S3.",
      "GuardDuty analyzes its own independent stream of flow log data. That data is not available for customers to query."] },

{ id: "d1-22", d: 1,
  q: "A company connects 40 VPCs in 12 accounts through one AWS Transit Gateway in a network account. The security team must record metadata about the IP traffic that passes through the transit gateway, including the source and destination transit gateway attachments. The data must be collected from the network account without changes in the 12 workload accounts.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a transit gateway flow log for the transit gateway in the network account. Publish the flow log to Amazon S3 or Amazon CloudWatch Logs.",
      "Create VPC flow logs for the subnets of each transit gateway attachment in the workload accounts. Publish the logs to Amazon S3 in the network account.",
      "Use Network Manager route analysis on the transit gateway in the network account. Export the route analysis results to Amazon S3 each hour.",
      "Create a VPC flow log in the network account for the VPC that hosts the transit gateway. Publish the flow log to Amazon S3."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Transit gateway flow logs capture IP traffic that goes through a transit gateway, including the attachments that it passes between. One flow log in the network account covers all of it.",
      "This needs changes in all 12 workload accounts, and VPC flow logs do not record which transit gateway attachments the traffic used.",
      "Route analysis checks the routing path between two endpoints. It does not record the traffic that actually flows.",
      "A transit gateway is not hosted in a VPC, so a VPC flow log in the network account does not see its traffic."] },

{ id: "d1-23", d: 1,
  q: "A company suspects that malware on some Amazon EC2 instances is resolving command and control domain names. The instances run in 15 VPCs in one account and use the Amazon Route 53 Resolver. The security team must record every DNS query that the instances send, including the domain name and the instance that sent it, and wants to set up the logging once for all 15 VPCs.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create one Route 53 Resolver query logging configuration. Associate it with all 15 VPCs, and send the logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs or Amazon S3.",
      "Turn on Route 53 public DNS query logging for each public hosted zone in the account. Send the logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs in us-east-1.",
      "Create VPC flow logs for the 15 VPCs with a custom format that adds the DNS query name field. Send the logs to Amazon S3.",
      "Create a Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall rule group that allows all domains with an alert action. Associate it with the 15 VPCs, and review the alerts."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Resolver query logging records DNS queries that resources in a VPC send to the Resolver, including the domain name and the source instance. One configuration can be associated with many VPCs.",
      "Public DNS query logging records queries that internet resolvers send for the company's own hosted zones, not what the instances look up.",
      "Flow logs have no field for DNS query names, so they cannot show the domains that were resolved.",
      "DNS Firewall alerts are written to Resolver query logs, so this design still needs query logging, and an allow-all rule group adds nothing."] },

{ id: "d1-24", d: 1,
  q: "A company uses AWS WAF to protect an Application Load Balancer for a payments API. The security team must keep a full record of every request that the web ACL evaluates, including the rule that terminated each request, for 1 year. The Authorization header and the card number query string parameter must never be written to the logs.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Enable web ACL logging to an Amazon S3 bucket with a 1-year lifecycle rule. Add redacted fields for the Authorization header and the query string.",
      "Enable web ACL logging to an Amazon S3 bucket with a 1-year lifecycle rule. Add a logging filter that drops the requests that have an Authorization header.",
      "Enable access logs on the Application Load Balancer to an Amazon S3 bucket with a 1-year lifecycle rule. Remove the Authorization header from the log format.",
      "Enable sampled requests for every rule in the web ACL. Export the sampled requests to an Amazon S3 bucket each day, and keep them for 1 year."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS WAF logs record every request that the web ACL evaluates, with the terminating rule. Redacted fields replace the values of the chosen header and query string before the logs are written.",
      "A logging filter drops whole requests from the logs, so the record would no longer be complete.",
      "Load balancer access logs have a fixed format and do not record which AWS WAF rule matched each request.",
      "Sampled requests are a small sample of recent traffic, not a full record of every request."] },

{ id: "d1-25", d: 1,
  q: "A company runs three Amazon EKS clusters for a payments platform. Investigators must be able to see every request to the Kubernetes API server, including the IAM principal or Kubernetes user that made it and whether the request was allowed. The records must be kept for 1 year in a central place, without agents on the worker nodes.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on the audit log type in EKS control plane logging for each cluster. Set a 1-year retention period on the cluster log groups in Amazon CloudWatch Logs.",
      "Turn on the authenticator log type in EKS control plane logging for each cluster. Set a 1-year retention period on the cluster log groups in Amazon CloudWatch Logs.",
      "Turn on the api log type in EKS control plane logging for each cluster. Set a 1-year retention period on the cluster log groups in Amazon CloudWatch Logs.",
      "Rely on the AWS CloudTrail management events for Amazon EKS in each account. Keep the events for 1 year, and search them for Kubernetes API requests."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Kubernetes audit logs record each request to the API server, the user or role behind it, and the decision. EKS control plane logging sends them to CloudWatch Logs without agents on the nodes.",
      "Authenticator logs record how IAM credentials were mapped to Kubernetes identities. They do not record every API request and its result.",
      "The api log type contains the API server's own component logs for troubleshooting. The audit log type is the record of individual requests and who made them.",
      "CloudTrail records calls to the Amazon EKS API, such as creating a cluster. It does not record requests to the Kubernetes API server inside the cluster."] },

{ id: "d1-26", d: 1,
  q: "During an incident, a security engineer must find every API call in the last 24 hours that failed with an AccessDenied or UnauthorizedOperation error, grouped by the IAM principal that made each call. AWS CloudTrail delivers the account's management events to an Amazon CloudWatch Logs log group and to an Amazon S3 bucket. The answer is needed within minutes, and no new infrastructure can be deployed during the incident.\n\nWhat should the security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Run a CloudWatch Logs Insights query on the log group for the last 24 hours. Filter errorCode for the two error values, and count the results by userIdentity.arn.",
      "Create a metric filter on the log group for the two error values in errorCode. Add userIdentity.arn as a dimension, and review the metric for the last 24 hours.",
      "Run a CloudWatch Logs Insights query on the log group for the last 24 hours. Filter eventName for the two error values, and count the results by sourceIPAddress.",
      "Create an AWS Glue crawler and an Amazon Athena table over the trail's S3 bucket. Query the table for the two error values, grouped by userIdentity.arn."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Logs Insights queries the existing log data in place. Filtering errorCode and counting by userIdentity.arn returns the failed calls for each principal in seconds, with nothing new to deploy.",
      "A metric filter counts only events that arrive after it is created, so it cannot report the last 24 hours.",
      "The error value is in the errorCode field, not eventName, and grouping by source IP address does not identify the principal.",
      "A crawler and a table are new infrastructure that takes time to set up, which the incident rules out."] },

{ id: "d1-27", d: 1,
  q: "A company stores 3 years of AWS CloudTrail logs from an organization trail for 40 accounts in an Amazon S3 bucket, under the default AWSLogs prefix structure. A security engineer runs ad hoc SQL queries that usually cover one account, one Region, and a few days. Each query must scan as little data as possible, and no one must add or repair partitions as new logs arrive.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an Amazon Athena table with partition projection on the account, Region, and date parts of the prefix. Filter every query on these projected columns.",
      "Create an Amazon Athena table that is partitioned on account, Region, and date. Run MSCK REPAIR TABLE before each query to load the new partitions.",
      "Create an Amazon Athena table with partition projection on the account, Region, and date parts of the prefix. Filter each query on the eventTime column only.",
      "Create an Amazon Athena table without partitions over the whole bucket. Convert the bucket's objects to Apache Parquet each night to reduce the data scanned."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Partition projection calculates partitions from a configured pattern, so new days are queryable without adding partitions. Filtering on the projected account, Region, and date columns limits each scan to the matching prefixes.",
      "Repairing partitions before queries is the maintenance that the company wants to avoid, and the CloudTrail prefix is not in the key=value form that MSCK REPAIR TABLE expects.",
      "eventTime is a column inside the files, not a partition, so Athena would still read every partition.",
      "Without partitions, every query reads the whole bucket, and the nightly conversion is a job to run and maintain."] },

{ id: "d1-28", d: 1,
  q: "A security operations team needs near real-time search, correlation, and dashboards across AWS WAF logs, VPC Flow Logs, and application logs that all arrive in Amazon CloudWatch Logs. Analysts must be able to search events within a minute of arrival and keep 30 days of searchable data. The team wants a managed service and does not want to install or patch search software.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Stream the logs with CloudWatch Logs subscription filters to Amazon OpenSearch Service. Build the correlation searches and dashboards in OpenSearch Dashboards.",
      "Export the log groups to Amazon S3 every 12 hours. Query the exported files with Amazon Athena, and build the dashboards in Amazon QuickSight.",
      "Run an Elasticsearch cluster on Amazon EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group. Stream the logs to the cluster with CloudWatch Logs subscription filters.",
      "Send each log source to its own Amazon Kinesis data stream. Have the analysts read the streams with the Kinesis Data Viewer during investigations."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["OpenSearch Service is a managed search and analytics service that indexes log data in near real time. Subscription filters stream the logs to it, and OpenSearch Dashboards supports correlation searches and dashboards.",
      "Exports every 12 hours cannot make events searchable within a minute.",
      "A self-managed cluster on EC2 means installing and patching the search software, which the team wants to avoid.",
      "Kinesis streams carry the data but do not index, search, or correlate it."] },

{ id: "d1-29", d: 1,
  q: "A company uses Amazon Security Lake in a delegated administrator account. A third-party firewall writes about 2 TB of logs each day in its own JSON format to an Amazon S3 bucket. The security team must query the firewall logs together with the AWS log sources in Security Lake by using the same schema and the same Athena tables, without changing the firewall's configuration.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create a custom source in Security Lake for the firewall, with an IAM role that is allowed to write to the source location.",
      "Use an AWS Glue job or AWS Lambda function to convert the firewall logs to OCSF in Apache Parquet and write them to the custom source location.",
      "Configure the firewall to write its logs directly to the Security Lake bucket in their original JSON format.",
      "Add the firewall's S3 bucket as a data access subscriber in Security Lake in each Region that the firewall uses.",
      "Create a custom source for the firewall, and copy the JSON files to the custom source location without any conversion."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Security Lake accepts third-party data through custom sources. Each custom source has a location in the Security Lake bucket and an IAM role that is used to write to it.",
      "Data from a custom source must already be in the Open Cybersecurity Schema Framework (OCSF) and stored as Apache Parquet. A conversion step outside the firewall makes the logs match the AWS sources.",
      "This changes the firewall's configuration, and raw JSON does not match the OCSF schema of the other sources.",
      "Subscribers consume Security Lake data. A subscriber cannot add a new log source.",
      "Unconverted JSON in the custom source location does not follow OCSF, so it cannot be queried with the same tables."] },

{ id: "d1-30", d: 1,
  q: "A company's security team wants shared dashboards that combine Amazon CloudWatch metrics with Amazon Athena query results over Amazon Security Lake data. About 40 team members must sign in through AWS IAM Identity Center, and their existing groups must decide who can edit and who can only view dashboards. The team does not want to patch or scale dashboard servers.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create an Amazon Managed Grafana workspace with IAM Identity Center authentication. Map the groups to editor and viewer roles, and add the CloudWatch and Athena data sources.",
      "Create an Amazon Managed Grafana workspace with SAML authentication through a separate identity provider. Create a local Grafana user for each of the 40 team members.",
      "Run Grafana on Amazon EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group behind a load balancer. Configure IAM Identity Center as the SAML identity provider for the servers.",
      "Create Amazon QuickSight dashboards with Athena as the data source. Add the CloudWatch metrics to QuickSight as a second direct data source for the dashboards."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Amazon Managed Grafana runs Grafana without servers to manage. It supports IAM Identity Center sign-in with group-based roles, and it has data sources for CloudWatch and Athena.",
      "A separate identity provider and local users ignore the requirement to sign in through IAM Identity Center with the existing groups.",
      "Self-managed Grafana servers must be patched and scaled by the team.",
      "QuickSight can query Athena, but it has no direct data source for CloudWatch metrics."] },

{ id: "d1-31", d: 1,
  q: "Twelve applications write logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs in different formats. The security team needs each log event converted to one JSON structure with the user, action, and resource fields, and needs the password and token fields removed. The converted events must be stored in Amazon S3 within 5 minutes, and the sensitive fields must never be stored in S3.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create subscription filters that send the log events to Amazon Data Firehose. Use a Lambda data transformation to convert each event and remove the fields before delivery.",
      "Create subscription filters that send the log events to Amazon Data Firehose with delivery to S3. Use S3 Object Lambda to convert each event and remove the fields when it is read.",
      "Create an export task for each log group every 5 minutes to an S3 bucket. Run an AWS Glue job on each export that converts the events and removes the fields.",
      "Create a metric filter for each log format that extracts the user, action, and resource fields. Send the extracted values to S3 through a CloudWatch metric stream."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Subscription filters stream log events in near real time to Firehose. A Lambda transformation converts each record and removes the sensitive fields before Firehose writes to S3, so the fields are never stored.",
      "Object Lambda changes data only when it is read, so the stored objects would still contain the passwords and tokens.",
      "Export tasks write the raw events to S3 before the job runs, and only one export task can run at a time in an account.",
      "Metric filters produce numeric metrics, not the log events, so the converted events would not exist."] },

{ id: "d1-32", d: 1,
  q: "A company uses Amazon Security Lake in a delegated administrator account. A third-party SIEM that runs in another AWS account must be notified when new data arrives and must copy the new objects into its own storage. A separate analytics team in a third account must run SQL queries with Amazon Athena on the same data in place, without copying it and without direct access to the S3 bucket.\n\nWhich subscriber configuration will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a data access subscriber for the SIEM account with notifications for new objects. Create a query access subscriber for the analytics account.",
      "Create a query access subscriber for the SIEM account with notifications for new objects. Create a data access subscriber for the analytics account.",
      "Create a data access subscriber for the SIEM account with notifications for new objects. Create a second data access subscriber for the analytics account.",
      "Create a query access subscriber for both accounts. Have the SIEM run scheduled Athena queries and copy the query results into its own storage."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A data access subscriber is notified of new objects and reads them from the S3 bucket, which suits a SIEM that copies data. A query access subscriber queries the data in place through AWS Lake Formation and Athena, without access to the bucket.",
      "This reverses the two subscriber types, so neither consumer gets the access that it needs.",
      "A data access subscriber reads objects directly from the bucket, which the analytics team must not do.",
      "Scheduled queries do not notify the SIEM when data arrives, and copying query results is not copying the new objects."] },

{ id: "d1-33", d: 1,
  q: "A company exposes a REST API through Amazon API Gateway for 300 partner integrations that sign their requests with IAM credentials. The security team must record the source IP address, the IAM principal of the caller, the request path, and the status code of every request in Amazon CloudWatch Logs, as one JSON line for each request.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Enable access logging for the API stage. Use a JSON log format that includes $context.identity.sourceIp, $context.identity.userArn, $context.path, and $context.status.",
      "Enable access logging for the API stage. Use a JSON log format that includes $context.identity.sourceIp, $context.authorizer.principalId, $context.path, and $context.status.",
      "Enable execution logging at the INFO level for the API stage. Search the execution log group for the caller, the path, and the status of each request.",
      "Enable AWS X-Ray active tracing for the API stage. Export the trace segment for each request, with the caller and status code, to CloudWatch Logs."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Access logging writes one entry for each request in a format that the team defines. With IAM authorization, $context.identity.userArn holds the caller's principal, and the other variables hold the IP address, path, and status.",
      "$context.authorizer.principalId is set by Lambda authorizers. With IAM authorization it does not contain the caller's IAM principal.",
      "Execution logs are detailed troubleshooting logs with many lines for each request, not one JSON line with the required fields.",
      "X-Ray samples requests, so it does not record every request, and traces are not access logs."] },

{ id: "d1-34", d: 1,
  q: "A company serves a web application through Amazon CloudFront, and most responses come from the edge cache. The security team must analyze the client IP address, URI, user agent, and edge result type of every viewer request for 90 days to investigate scraping. The origin is an Application Load Balancer that already has access logging enabled.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Enable CloudFront standard logging for the distribution to an Amazon S3 bucket. Add a lifecycle rule that deletes the log files after 90 days.",
      "Use the access logs of the Application Load Balancer that already exist. Keep the log files in Amazon S3 for 90 days with a lifecycle rule.",
      "Enable CloudFront real-time logs at a 1 percent sampling rate to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Store the records in Amazon S3 for 90 days.",
      "Enable sampled requests on the AWS WAF web ACL of the distribution. Export the sampled requests to Amazon S3 each day for 90 days."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudFront standard logs record every viewer request with the client IP address, URI, user agent, and edge result type, including requests served from cache. A lifecycle rule keeps them for 90 days.",
      "The load balancer sees only cache misses, so the requests that CloudFront serves from the cache would be missing.",
      "A 1 percent sampling rate records only some requests, not every viewer request.",
      "Sampled requests are a small sample of recent traffic, not every request."] },

{ id: "d1-35", d: 1,
  q: "A company stores AWS CloudTrail logs and VPC Flow Logs in an Amazon S3 bucket in a log archive account. A regulation requires that no one, including the root user and the administrators of the log archive account, can delete or overwrite any log file for 1 year. After 1 year, the logs must be removed automatically, and the storage for expired data must not keep growing.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Enable S3 Object Lock on the bucket with a default retention period of 1 year in compliance mode.",
      "Add an S3 Lifecycle rule that expires current versions after 1 year and permanently deletes noncurrent versions.",
      "Enable S3 Object Lock on the bucket with a default retention period of 1 year in governance mode.",
      "Place an S3 Object Lock legal hold on every log object, and remove each legal hold after 1 year.",
      "Add an S3 Lifecycle rule that expires current versions after 1 year, and keep every noncurrent version."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["In compliance mode, no user, including the root user, can overwrite or delete a protected object version or shorten its retention until the period ends.",
      "Object Lock uses versioning, so expiring current versions only adds delete markers. The rule must also delete noncurrent versions to remove the data.",
      "In governance mode, users with the s3:BypassGovernanceRetention permission can delete protected objects, so administrators could still remove logs.",
      "Legal holds have no end date and can be removed by users with the s3:PutObjectLegalHold permission, so they neither expire nor stop administrators.",
      "Keeping noncurrent versions leaves the old data in place, so storage keeps growing after the logs expire."] },

{ id: "d1-36", d: 1,
  q: "A security engineer creates an AWS CloudTrail trail in account A that delivers to a new Amazon S3 bucket in a log archive account. The trail status shows a delivery error, and no log files appear in the bucket. The bucket uses SSE-S3 encryption, has S3 Block Public Access turned on, and has a bucket policy that allows access only to the log archive account's administrators.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The bucket policy does not allow the cloudtrail.amazonaws.com service principal to call s3:GetBucketAcl and s3:PutObject for account A's AWSLogs path.",
      "The bucket policy does not allow the root principal of account A to call s3:PutObject on the AWSLogs path, which CloudTrail uses to write the log files.",
      "S3 Block Public Access on the bucket stops CloudTrail from writing log files, because the CloudTrail service principal counts as a public principal.",
      "The bucket uses SSE-S3, and CloudTrail cannot deliver log files to a bucket in another account unless the bucket uses SSE-KMS with a customer managed key."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudTrail writes log files as the cloudtrail.amazonaws.com service principal. The bucket policy must let it check the bucket ACL and put objects in the AWSLogs path for the account, usually with an aws:SourceArn condition for the trail.",
      "CloudTrail does not write with the credentials of account A's principals, so a grant to account A's root principal does not allow delivery.",
      "A bucket policy statement for a specific service principal is not public, so Block Public Access does not block CloudTrail.",
      "SSE-S3 is the default encryption for CloudTrail log files, including in another account, so it does not block delivery."] },

{ id: "d1-37", d: 1,
  q: "A company runs Amazon EC2 instances in private subnets that have no route to the internet or to a NAT gateway. The Amazon CloudWatch agent is installed and running, and the instance profile includes the CloudWatchAgentServerPolicy managed policy. No logs appear in CloudWatch Logs, and the agent log shows connection timeouts to the CloudWatch Logs endpoint.\n\nWhich combination of steps will resolve this issue? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create an interface VPC endpoint for CloudWatch Logs in the private subnets with private DNS enabled.",
      "Allow inbound HTTPS from the instances' security group in the security group of the endpoint.",
      "Create a gateway VPC endpoint for CloudWatch Logs, and add it to the route tables of the private subnets.",
      "Attach the CloudWatchAgentAdminPolicy managed policy to the instance profile, and restart the agent.",
      "Allow outbound HTTPS from the endpoint's security group to the security group of the instances."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Timeouts mean the agent cannot reach the service. An interface VPC endpoint with private DNS lets the agent resolve the normal CloudWatch Logs endpoint name to private addresses in the VPC.",
      "The endpoint's network interfaces accept only traffic that their security group allows, so it must allow HTTPS from the instances.",
      "Gateway endpoints exist only for Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB, so this endpoint cannot be created.",
      "The instance profile already has the permissions that the agent needs, and permissions do not fix a network timeout.",
      "Security groups are stateful, so responses are allowed. The missing rule is inbound HTTPS on the endpoint."] },

{ id: "d1-38", d: 1,
  q: "A company's AWS Lambda function runs without errors and returns correct results, but no log group or log events appear in Amazon CloudWatch Logs for the function. The function uses a custom execution role that the security team wrote by following least privilege. For CloudWatch Logs, the role allows only logs:PutLogEvents on the function's log group and its log streams.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The role does not allow logs:CreateLogGroup and logs:CreateLogStream, so Lambda cannot create the function's log group and log streams.",
      "The function's resource-based policy does not allow the logs.amazonaws.com service principal to invoke the function and collect its output.",
      "The function does not have AWS X-Ray active tracing enabled, which Lambda needs before it can send the function's logs to CloudWatch Logs.",
      "The function's log format is set to JSON, which Lambda can send only to a log group that an administrator creates in advance by hand."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Lambda sends logs by using the execution role. The log group does not exist yet, and without logs:CreateLogGroup and logs:CreateLogStream, Lambda cannot create it or its streams, so PutLogEvents never has a target.",
      "CloudWatch Logs does not invoke functions to collect logs. Lambda pushes the logs with the execution role.",
      "Tracing and logging are separate features, and logging does not depend on X-Ray.",
      "The JSON log format works with log groups that Lambda creates, as long as the role allows it."] },

{ id: "d1-39", d: 1,
  q: "A security engineer enables CloudWatch execution logging and access logging for a REST API stage in Amazon API Gateway in eu-west-1. When the engineer saves the settings, API Gateway returns an error that says that a CloudWatch Logs role ARN must be set in the account settings. APIs in us-east-1 in the same account already log without errors.\n\nWhich combination of steps will resolve this issue? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create an IAM role that trusts apigateway.amazonaws.com and has the AmazonAPIGatewayPushToCloudWatchLogs managed policy.",
      "Set the role's ARN as the CloudWatch log role in the API Gateway account settings for the eu-west-1 Region.",
      "Add a resource policy to the API that allows logs.amazonaws.com to write the execution logs for the stage.",
      "Rely on the CloudWatch log role that is set in us-east-1, which API Gateway applies to every Region.",
      "Create the stage's log groups by hand, and grant the API's callers permission to write log events to them."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["API Gateway writes logs by assuming a role that trusts apigateway.amazonaws.com. The managed policy grants the CloudWatch Logs permissions that it needs.",
      "The CloudWatch log role is an account setting for each Region, so eu-west-1 needs its own setting even though us-east-1 has one.",
      "A resource policy controls who can invoke the API. It does not let API Gateway write logs.",
      "The setting does not carry across Regions, which is why us-east-1 works and eu-west-1 fails.",
      "API Gateway writes the logs, not the callers, and it still needs the role in the account settings."] },

{ id: "d1-40", d: 1,
  q: "A security engineer enables access logs for an Application Load Balancer in eu-west-1 and selects an existing Amazon S3 bucket in the same Region. The load balancer reports an access denied error for the bucket, and no logs are delivered. The bucket uses SSE-S3 encryption, and its bucket policy allows access only to the account's administrators.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The bucket policy does not allow the Elastic Load Balancing log delivery principal for the Region to put objects in the bucket's log prefix.",
      "The bucket policy does not allow the load balancer's own IAM role to put objects in the log prefix, which the load balancer uses for log delivery.",
      "The bucket uses SSE-S3, and Elastic Load Balancing delivers access logs only to buckets that use SSE-KMS with a customer managed key.",
      "The load balancer's security group does not allow outbound HTTPS to the Amazon S3 endpoint, which the access log delivery uses."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Elastic Load Balancing writes access logs as a log delivery principal for the Region. The bucket policy must allow that principal to put objects in the log prefix, or delivery fails with access denied.",
      "Load balancers do not have IAM roles. Delivery uses the Elastic Load Balancing log delivery principal.",
      "Access logs support SSE-S3 on the destination bucket. SSE-KMS with a customer managed key is not supported for these logs.",
      "Log delivery is done by the service, not through the load balancer's network interfaces, so its security group is not involved."] },

{ id: "d1-41", d: 1,
  q: "A security engineer creates a VPC flow log that publishes to an Amazon CloudWatch Logs log group. The flow log status shows an access error, and no log streams are created. The engineer selected an IAM role whose permissions policy allows logs:CreateLogGroup, logs:CreateLogStream, logs:PutLogEvents, logs:DescribeLogGroups, and logs:DescribeLogStreams on all resources.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The role's trust policy does not allow the vpc-flow-logs.amazonaws.com service principal to assume the role.",
      "The role's trust policy does not allow the ec2.amazonaws.com service principal to assume the role, which flow logs use for delivery.",
      "The log group must be created before the flow log, because flow logs cannot create a log group even with the logs:CreateLogGroup permission.",
      "The VPC has no interface VPC endpoint for CloudWatch Logs, which flow logs need to deliver records from instances in private subnets."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["VPC Flow Logs publishes to CloudWatch Logs by assuming the role that the flow log names. The permissions are complete, so the problem is the trust policy, which must allow the vpc-flow-logs.amazonaws.com service principal.",
      "Flow logs are delivered by the VPC Flow Logs service principal, not by EC2, so trusting ec2.amazonaws.com does not help.",
      "Flow logs can create the log group when the role allows logs:CreateLogGroup.",
      "The service delivers flow logs outside the VPC's network path, so no VPC endpoint is needed."] },

{ id: "d1-42", d: 1,
  q: "A security engineer creates an Amazon EventBridge rule on the default event bus to start an AWS Lambda function for every new Amazon GuardDuty finding. GuardDuty generates findings in the same Region, but the rule's MatchedEvents metric stays at 0. The rule's event pattern matches the source guardduty.amazonaws.com and the detail-type GuardDuty Finding.\n\nWhat should the security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Change the source in the event pattern to aws.guardduty, and keep the detail-type GuardDuty Finding.",
      "Change the detail-type in the event pattern to GuardDuty Findings, and keep the source guardduty.amazonaws.com.",
      "Move the rule to a custom event bus, and add a resource policy that allows GuardDuty to put events on the bus.",
      "Add a resource-based policy to the Lambda function that allows events.amazonaws.com to invoke the function."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Events from AWS services use a source value in the form aws.service. GuardDuty findings arrive on the default bus with the source aws.guardduty and the detail-type GuardDuty Finding, so the current source never matches.",
      "The detail-type is already correct, and the source would still not match.",
      "GuardDuty sends findings to the default event bus, not to custom buses.",
      "A missing invoke permission shows up as failed invocations after a match. A MatchedEvents value of 0 means the pattern itself is wrong."] },

{ id: "d1-43", d: 1,
  q: "An Amazon EventBridge rule sends AWS Security Hub CSPM findings to an Amazon SNS topic in the same account. After the security team enables server-side encryption on the topic with a customer managed AWS KMS key, the rule's FailedInvocations metric increases and no notifications arrive. The topic's access policy still allows events.amazonaws.com to publish.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Update the key policy to allow the events.amazonaws.com service principal to use kms:GenerateDataKey and kms:Decrypt with the key.",
      "Update the key policy to allow the sns.amazonaws.com service principal to use kms:GenerateDataKey and kms:Decrypt with the key.",
      "Replace the customer managed key with the AWS managed key for Amazon SNS, and keep the topic access policy as it is.",
      "Update the topic access policy to allow the securityhub.amazonaws.com service principal to publish to the topic."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The service that publishes to an encrypted topic must be able to use its KMS key. Here EventBridge publishes, so the key policy must allow events.amazonaws.com to call kms:GenerateDataKey and kms:Decrypt.",
      "The publisher is EventBridge, so a grant to the SNS service principal does not let EventBridge publish encrypted messages.",
      "The policy of the AWS managed key cannot be changed to allow EventBridge, so publishing would still fail.",
      "Security Hub CSPM does not publish to the topic. EventBridge does, and the access policy already allows it."] },

{ id: "d1-44", d: 1,
  q: "A company uses AWS Security Hub CSPM and Amazon GuardDuty in a single account in us-east-1 and eu-west-1. The security team reviews findings in Security Hub CSPM in us-east-1. GuardDuty findings from us-east-1 appear there, but findings from eu-west-1 never do. GuardDuty is enabled in both Regions, and Security Hub CSPM was enabled only in us-east-1.\n\nWhich combination of steps will resolve this issue? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Enable Security Hub CSPM in eu-west-1 to receive the GuardDuty findings from that Region.",
      "Configure cross-Region aggregation with us-east-1 as the home Region and eu-west-1 as a linked Region.",
      "Create an EventBridge rule in eu-west-1 that sends GuardDuty findings to the default event bus in us-east-1.",
      "Turn on the GuardDuty integration in Security Hub CSPM in us-east-1 for findings from all Regions.",
      "Set us-east-1 as the GuardDuty findings Region, and send findings from eu-west-1 to it automatically."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["GuardDuty sends findings to Security Hub CSPM in the same Region, so Security Hub CSPM must be enabled in eu-west-1 to receive them.",
      "Cross-Region aggregation then copies findings from the linked Region to the home Region, so the team sees eu-west-1 findings in us-east-1.",
      "Forwarded events would arrive as events, not as findings in Security Hub CSPM, so they would not appear in the findings list.",
      "Product integrations work within each Region. The us-east-1 integration cannot pull findings from eu-west-1.",
      "GuardDuty has no setting that sends findings from one Region to another."] },

{ id: "d1-45", d: 1,
  q: "A company enables AWS Security Hub CSPM and the AWS Foundational Security Best Practices standard in a new account. After two days, many controls still show no data, and their findings never update. A few controls that check account-level settings do report results. The company has not configured any other AWS security service in the account.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["AWS Config is not recording resources, and most controls are evaluated through AWS Config rules that Security Hub CSPM creates.",
      "Amazon GuardDuty is not enabled in the account, and most controls evaluate resources by using GuardDuty findings as their input data.",
      "The account has no AWS CloudTrail trail, and most controls evaluate resources by reading the management events from a CloudTrail trail.",
      "The standard needs 30 days of AWS Config history in a new account before most controls can report a status for the account's resources."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Security Hub CSPM evaluates most controls through service-linked AWS Config rules. Without AWS Config recording the resources, those controls have nothing to evaluate.",
      "GuardDuty sends threat findings to Security Hub CSPM, but controls do not depend on GuardDuty.",
      "Controls evaluate resource configuration through AWS Config, not by reading a trail, and a few CloudTrail controls report on the absence of a trail.",
      "Controls start reporting soon after AWS Config records the resources. There is no 30-day wait."] },

{ id: "d1-46", d: 1,
  q: "During an investigation, a security engineer finds that the company's AWS CloudTrail trail has no events for API calls that an attacker made in ap-southeast-2. The trail was created in us-east-1 years ago by an AWS CLI script, and it does contain IAM and AWS STS calls. CloudTrail Event history in ap-southeast-2 shows the attacker's calls from the last 90 days.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The trail is a single-Region trail. It records global service events, but it does not record management events from ap-southeast-2.",
      "The trail's S3 bucket is in us-east-1, and CloudTrail cannot deliver events from other Regions to a bucket that is in a different Region.",
      "The attacker used temporary credentials from AWS STS, and CloudTrail does not record API calls that are signed with temporary credentials.",
      "The trail has an advanced event selector that excludes ap-southeast-2, which is the default for trails that are created with the AWS CLI."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A trail created with the AWS CLI is a single-Region trail unless it is set as multi-Region. It still records global service events such as IAM and AWS STS, but not management events in other Regions.",
      "A multi-Region trail delivers events from every Region to one bucket, wherever the bucket is.",
      "CloudTrail records calls made with temporary credentials, and Event history in ap-southeast-2 already shows these calls.",
      "Advanced event selectors cannot exclude Regions, and the AWS CLI does not add selectors by default."] },

{ id: "d1-47", d: 1,
  q: "A security engineer updates the Amazon CloudWatch agent configuration in Parameter Store to collect an additional audit log file from 300 instances. The instances are managed by AWS Systems Manager, and the agent was installed and configured six months ago with a one-time Run Command. Several days after the update, the instances still send only the log files from the old configuration.\n\nWhat should the security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Run the AmazonCloudWatch-ManageAgent document with the configure action and the updated Parameter Store configuration.",
      "Run the AWS-ConfigureAWSPackage document to install the latest version of the CloudWatch agent again on all of the instances.",
      "Attach the CloudWatchAgentAdminPolicy managed policy to the instance profile. The agent then polls Parameter Store for changes.",
      "Update the parameter again with a new version label. The agent loads the newest labeled version of the parameter within 24 hours."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The agent reads its configuration only when it is configured. Running AmazonCloudWatch-ManageAgent with the configure action fetches the new configuration from Parameter Store and restarts the agent with it.",
      "Reinstalling the agent package does not load a new configuration into the agent.",
      "The admin policy lets an agent write configurations to Parameter Store. Running agents do not poll for changes.",
      "The agent does not watch parameter versions or labels, so it keeps the configuration that it loaded."] },

{ id: "d1-48", d: 1,
  q: "Amazon Macie reports that it cannot analyze many objects in an Amazon S3 bucket. The objects are encrypted with SSE-KMS by using a customer managed AWS KMS key in the same account. Macie analyzes the objects that use SSE-S3 in the same bucket without errors, and the bucket policy does not deny any access to Macie.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Update the key policy to allow the Macie service-linked role to use kms:Decrypt with the customer managed key.",
      "Update the key policy to allow the macie.amazonaws.com service principal to use kms:Decrypt with the customer managed key.",
      "Add a bucket policy statement that allows the Macie service-linked role to call s3:GetObject on all objects in the bucket.",
      "Change the bucket's default encryption to SSE-S3. Macie can then analyze the existing objects that use SSE-KMS."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Macie reads objects by using its service-linked role. For objects encrypted with a customer managed key, the key policy must allow that role to call kms:Decrypt.",
      "Macie decrypts objects as its service-linked role, so the key policy must name that role, not the service principal.",
      "S3 access is not the problem, because Macie already reads the SSE-S3 objects in the same bucket. The missing permission is on the KMS key.",
      "Default encryption applies only to new objects. Existing objects stay encrypted with the customer managed key."] },

{ id: "d1-49", d: 1,
  q: "A company must be notified within minutes whenever any security group in its account allows inbound SSH from 0.0.0.0/0 or ::/0. Each notification must identify the security group that changed. AWS Config already records all resource types in the account, and an Amazon SNS topic with the security team's email subscription exists.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Enable the restricted-ssh AWS Config managed rule, which runs when a security group's configuration changes.",
      "Create an EventBridge rule for compliance changes of the rule to NON_COMPLIANT, with the SNS topic as the target.",
      "Enable the restricted-common-ports AWS Config managed rule with port 22 removed from the rule parameters.",
      "Create an EventBridge rule for every AWS Config configuration item change, with the SNS topic as the target.",
      "Run the vpc-sg-open-only-to-authorized-ports rule on a 24-hour periodic schedule, and email its results."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["The restricted-ssh rule runs on security group configuration changes and marks a group as noncompliant when it allows unrestricted inbound SSH.",
      "AWS Config sends each compliance change to EventBridge with the resource ID, so the rule can alert within minutes and name the security group.",
      "With port 22 removed from its parameters, restricted-common-ports no longer checks SSH at all.",
      "Every configuration change of every resource would trigger an email, and the event says nothing about whether SSH is open.",
      "A 24-hour periodic schedule cannot notify within minutes."] },

{ id: "d1-50", d: 1,
  q: "A company has an Amazon GuardDuty delegated administrator account for its organization of 90 accounts. The security team must send every new GuardDuty finding from all member accounts to an external SIEM that accepts events at an HTTPS endpoint with an API key in a request header. The solution must not create resources in member accounts and must not use custom code.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select THREE.)",
  o: ["Create an EventBridge rule in the delegated administrator account that matches GuardDuty findings.",
      "Create an EventBridge connection that stores the SIEM's API key for the request header.",
      "Create an EventBridge API destination for the SIEM endpoint that uses the connection, and set it as the rule's target.",
      "Create an EventBridge rule in each member account that forwards GuardDuty findings to the SIEM endpoint.",
      "Create an AWS Lambda function that posts each finding to the SIEM endpoint, and set it as the rule's target."],
  a: [0, 1, 2],
  r: ["GuardDuty publishes member account findings to EventBridge in the administrator account, so one rule there matches findings from the whole organization.",
      "An EventBridge connection holds the authorization details, such as an API key header, and stores the secret in AWS Secrets Manager.",
      "An API destination sends matched events to an HTTPS endpoint with the connection's authorization and with retries, so no code is needed.",
      "Rules in every member account are the per-account resources that the company wants to avoid.",
      "A Lambda function is custom code, which the company does not want."] },
]);
